From 7558b1b821623e6a2837b7caa2a9339d76d67210 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tyler Baker <tyler.baker@oss.qualcomm.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 09:37:07 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] drm/msm/dpu: do not tear down the kms twice when hw_init
 fails

When dpu_kms_hw_init() fails, its error path calls _dpu_kms_hw_destroy(),
which finalizes the global private object. msm_drm_kms_init() then
returns the error to msm_drm_init(), whose unwind runs
msm_drm_kms_uninit() and so dpu_kms_destroy(), which calls
_dpu_kms_hw_destroy() a second time. drm_atomic_private_obj_fini() does
a list_del() on the object, so the second call writes through the
poisoned list pointers:

  [drm] no IOMMU, bailing out
  [drm:dpu_kms_hw_init:1183] [dpu error]dpu_kms_mmu_init failed: -19
  *ERROR* kms hw init failed: -19
  Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address dead000000000108
  pc : drm_atomic_private_obj_fini+0x24/0x6c
  lr : dpu_kms_destroy+0x58/0xdc
   dpu_kms_destroy+0x58/0xdc
   msm_drm_kms_uninit+0xac/0xd8
   msm_drm_uninit.isra.0+0x4c/0xcc
   msm_drm_init+0x118/0x240

The Oops is taken in the deferred probe worker, so no further deferred
probe completes in that boot. It is seen on the Eliza MTP as a Xen dom0,
where the display streams sit behind an SMMU that Xen drives and dom0
has no IOMMU for the DPU. hw_init normally succeeds, which hid the
double teardown.

Leave the teardown to dpu_kms_destroy(), which every hw_init failure
reaches through msm_drm_kms_uninit(), and only drop the runtime PM
reference in the error path.

Signed-off-by: Tyler Baker <tyler.baker@oss.qualcomm.com>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.c | 2 --
 1 file changed, 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.c
index b7c6602af8bc0..7fa1e01e09101 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.c
@@ -1314,8 +1314,6 @@ static int dpu_kms_hw_init(struct msm_kms *kms)
 err_pm_put:
 	pm_runtime_put_sync(&dpu_kms->pdev->dev);
 error:
-	_dpu_kms_hw_destroy(dpu_kms);
-
 	return rc;
 }
 
